From 4efedc8abdf4a39a20c7cbd911f092d245cbe68e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: legsmechanical <joshgaine@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 23:22:44 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] test(shadow): pin edit-CC press/release routing; extract
 shadow_edit_cc_route
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The subtle part of claims_edit_ccs is not "a claimed button reaches the module" —
it is that press and release always reach the SAME consumer even when the claim
flips while the button is physically held. Both failure directions are silent:
a claim engaging mid-hold leaves Move believing the button is still down (it saw
the press, never the release); a claim dropping mid-hold hands Move an orphan
release it never saw pressed.

That case is awkward to produce on the device — it needs the on-screen module to
change mid-press — so pin it off-device instead. Extract the latch decision from
the shim into shadow_edit_cc_route() in shadow_constants.h, following the
corun_event_owner() precedent: routing predicates live in the header as one
source of truth that both the Move-firmware filter and the forward-to-shadow_ui
site call, so the two cannot drift.

Covers: unclaimed and claimed steady state, both mid-hold transitions, latch
re-arming on the next press (which is why it is never cleared on release), the
read-only query used by the later forward site, and per-button independence.
Verified the test fails when the helper is mutated to route on the live claim
instead of the latch — 4 checks trip, all the ones that should.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XX8gDJhHpYErLEwJsV5jPm
